    "continuity": "If met: VB-03 — accurate underhand serve. If not: return to catch, shorten distance, and lower net.",
    "bankLink": "Sport: Volleyball. Age: 9–11. Equipment: 12–16 balls, net or rope, 30 cones, bibs.\nTags: volleyball, overhead set, net games, school PE, foundation.\nSkills: overhead set, hand shape, moving under the ball, leg drive, support movement, calling.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: overhead set, finger pads, hand window, forehead contact, push from the legs, set height, support movement, target zone.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate–high. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up through movement and self-contact.\nBuild the skill without pressure.\nAdd a decision and small game.\nClose with evidence and next step.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? The set is the contact that turns a dug ball into an attack; without it a team can only return the ball, never build a point, and the three-touch rally that defines volleyball is impossible.\nWhat develops? A soft finger-pad contact and a stable hand shape, moving the feet to get under a falling ball, extension through the legs rather than the arms, and the habit of supporting the next contact after making one.\nWhy not skip? Students who never learn the hand shape set with flat palms or double-contact every ball, both of which are faults; jamming stiff fingers into a falling ball is also a real source of finger injuries, which a taught hand shape helps prevent.",
