    "bankLink": "Sport: Volleyball. Age: 9–11. Equipment: 12–16 balls, net or rope, 30 cones, bibs.\nTags: volleyball, underhand serve, accuracy, net games, school PE.\nSkills: underhand serve, pendulum arm swing, contact point, weight transfer, target selection, force control.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: underhand serve, held-ball contact, straight arm swing, step to the target, target zone, serve height, accuracy before power, serving signal.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate–high. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up through movement and self-contact.\nBuild the skill without pressure.\nAdd a decision and small game.\nClose with evidence and next step.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? The serve is the only closed skill in volleyball — nobody interferes with it — so it is the fairest chance in the sport for any student to succeed, and no rally can begin without one.\nWhat develops? A repeatable pendulum swing from a stable stance, weight transfer towards a chosen target, calibration of force to distance, and the self-discipline of serving only on a signal.\nWhy not skip? A class that cannot serve cannot start a rally, so games stall before they begin and the lesson becomes a queue; students who invent a serve also tend to throw the ball up and swing hard, which is both inaccurate and the least safe action on a shared court.",
