    "continuity": "If met: VB-05 — three-contact sequence. If not: return to catch, shorten distance, and lower net.",
    "bankLink": "Sport: Volleyball. Age: 10–12. Equipment: 12–16 balls, net or rope, 30 cones, bibs.\nTags: volleyball, serve reception, platform angle, net games, school PE.\nSkills: serve reception, reading flight, footwork behind the ball, platform angling, calling, first-contact control.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: serve reception, platform angle, setter zone, feet to the ball, first contact, reading flight, calling the ball, high pass.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate–high. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up through movement and self-contact.\nBuild the skill without pressure.\nAdd a decision and small game.\nClose with evidence and next step.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? The first contact sets the ceiling on everything that follows: a reception that does not reach the setter removes two of the team's three touches, so the quality of the reception limits every contact that follows it.\nWhat develops? Reading the flight of a served ball early, footwork that gets the body behind it, angling a still platform to redirect rather than return, and the calling that prevents collisions between two receivers.\nWhy not skip? Students who only ever return the serve straight back keep the ball in play but never build an attack, and the rally stays a two-team exchange of single contacts; two players converging silently on the same served ball is also a genuine collision risk.",
