    "bankLink": "Sport: Volleyball. Age: 11–13. Equipment: 12–16 balls, net or rope, 30 cones, bibs.\nTags: volleyball, three-contact rally, team sequence, net games, school PE.\nSkills: serve reception, overhead set, controlled send, role allocation, calling, base positioning.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: receive–set–send, three contacts, calling the ball, setter zone, role clarity, rally building, high second contact.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate–high. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up through movement and self-contact.\nBuild the skill without pressure.\nAdd a decision and small game.\nClose with evidence and next step.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? This is the lesson where volleyball becomes volleyball: three contacts by three players is the structure the whole sport is built on, and it cannot emerge from practising contacts separately.\nWhat develops? Role clarity under time pressure, the calling that prevents two players taking the same ball, judging the height and placement each contact needs in order to make the next one possible, and patience to build rather than return.\nWhy not skip? Classes that never rehearse the sequence return every ball on the first touch, which hands the advantage straight back; rallies stay one contact long, and every later tactical lesson has nothing to attach to.",
