    "adaptations": "Foundation: catch–throw, soft ball, low net. Intermediate: continuous contact. Advanced: small target or short time. Large group: six courts. Small: 2v2. Limited space: short court. Partial equipment: one ball per triad and rope. Varied needs: visual cue, strong markings, extra time, fixed partner.",
    "assessment": "Checks: open-hand contact, positioning under the ball, placement into open space, force control, communication. Success: 4–5 criteria. Partial: 2–3 with prompt. Not yet: 0–1 or unsafe; return to soft-ball catch–throw.",
    "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Did everyone contact the ball? Students: What did you see and where did you move? Next: revisit one criterion.",
    "continuity": "If met: VB-08 — net defence and backcourt coverage. If not: return to catch, shorten distance, and lower net.",
    "bankLink": "Sport: Volleyball. Age: 12–14. Equipment: 12–16 balls, net or rope, 30 cones, bibs.\nTags: volleyball, standing attack, placement, net games, school PE.\nSkills: open-hand attack, tip, positioning under the set, target selection, force control, disguise.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: standing attack, open hand, high elbow, tip, placement into space, controlled force, reading the defence, deep and short targets.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate–high. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up through movement and self-contact.\nBuild the skill without pressure.\nAdd a decision and small game.\nClose with evidence and next step.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? It is the point at which a team can finally win a rally rather than wait for the other side to make a mistake, and it establishes from the outset that an attack is aimed placement rather than maximum force.\nWhat develops? An open-hand striking pattern with a high elbow and firm wrist, moving under a set and attacking from balance, reading which space a defence has left, and disguising the choice between a hit and a tip.\nWhy not skip? Students who meet attacking for the first time in a free game hit as hard as they can at whoever is nearest, which is both ineffective and a genuine source of injury and fear on a shared court; teaching placement first sets the norm before power is available.",
